me-glipper — ongoing work
==========================

Being rebuilt from me-tracker (weight tracking) into a GLP-1 dose tracker, UI-first,
in vertical slices.

Done
----
- Slice 1: "Add dose" data-entry page, end to end.
  - Backend: Weight domain renamed to Dose. Dose entity/record/requests/collection,
    DoseRepository (Doses table: DoseId, UserId, TakenAt, MedicationName, DoseAmountMg,
    Deleted, Notes), DoseController (api/dose, api/doses). Backup updated. 42 tests green.
  - Frontend: DoseService + dose-input component (date, time, medication dropdown,
    dose mg, notes) on page 1.
- Slice 2: Dose history page (page 2). dose-records component — stacked cards per dose
  (date+time, medication, dose mg), delete-with-confirm, inline notes editing. Reuses
  DoseService.loadDoses / updateNotes / deleteDose. Frontend only (endpoints already existed).

Next: page 3 — "levels" page (keep the "report" name for now; TBD)
-----------------------------------------------------------------
Not a classic report. It should show, per medication:
  (a) estimated amount of drug remaining in the body — NUMERIC first, CHART later.
  (b) when the next dose is due.

- Pharmacokinetics spec: me-glipper-private/drug-level-spec.md (one-compartment model,
  first-order absorption + elimination, superposition of per-dose contributions; Instant
  decay vs Bateman modes; ka derived from Tmax; presets for sema/tirz/reta). Reviewed —
  model is sound; the full spec is somewhat gold-plated for step 1.
- (a) current amount remaining is a pure, clock-free calculation (caller passes the time).
  Next-dose-due (b) is a separate, simpler calc: per medication, last TakenAt + dosing
  interval (weekly for now).
- DECIDED: the calculator lives in the backend (pure C# class + endpoint). Lives under
  code/dotnet/Api/Controllers/Levels/ (DrugLevelCalculator, DrugParameters, DoseInput,
  DrugPresets), exposed via GET /api/levels (LevelsController).
- STEP 1 DONE: estimated level shown numerically, instant-decay model.
  Page 3 ("Estimated Levels") -> drug-levels component -> LevelsService -> GET /api/levels.
  Verified end to end (1 mg now + 1 mg 7 days ago => ~1.329 mg).
- STEP 2 DONE: all three drugs. Tirzepatide (t½ 117 h, F 0.80) + retatrutide (t½ 144 h,
  F 1.0) presets added; DrugPresets.All; LevelsController loops the presets and reports only
  medications the user has actually dosed (no zero rows); drug-levels has an empty state.
  60 tests green (calculator + preset + LevelsController integration tests).
- STEP 3 DONE: charts. GET /api/levels/series returns a shared time grid (first dose -> now,
  6h step + dose instants) with one aligned amounts array per dosed drug
  (DrugLevelCalculator.AmountRemainingSeriesMg). New page 4 ("Charts") with one uPlot chart
  per drug stacked vertically (drug-levels-charts + drug-level-chart), history-only with a
  1M/All range selector (client-side filter, like me-tracker). uPlot added as a dependency.
  63 tests green. NOTE: charts not yet eyeballed in a browser; landscape side-by-side layout
  is a possible later tweak.
- NEXT STEPS: add Bateman (first-order absorption) by swapping DrugLevelCalculator's
  per-dose function (AmountFromSingleDose) + ka/Tmax solver (the before/after the user wants
  to see on the chart); then next-dose-due per medication; then rearrange what lives on which
  page (page assignment is provisional).

Parked (re-enable in later slices)
----------------------------------
- Report feature: ReportController + ReportHandler are commented out (weight/BMI-specific,
  depended on the old Weight types). ReportHandlerTests + the report integration tests are
  commented out too. The new levels page replaces this slot — the old weight-report logic
  will be deleted rather than revived.
- Settings.AverageWeightWindowInDays is weight-report-specific; left in place, unused.

Other
-----
- Medication list is hard-coded (Semaglutide, Tirzepatide, Retatrutide); dose unit fixed
  to mg. Revisit if this needs to be configurable.
- CLAUDE.md: endpoint/field names updated to the dose domain in the data-entry notes;
  do a broader pass for any remaining weight-era references.
- Private backups (me-glipper-private/backups) hold old weight data in the old schema;
  they won't restore into the new Doses table.
